---

For those blissfully unaware, the source code for popular coding agent Claude Code got leaked in March of this year because a JavaScript source map was accidentally published to their npm package (whoops). Enclosed in this file was a reference to a storage bucket containing over 500,000 lines of unobfuscated code, revealing in broad detail the inner workings of Claude Code.

Amongst everything I found in the codebase, this gem of a function stood out to me the most:

```TypeScript
export function matchesNegativeKeyword(input: string): boolean {
    const lowerInput = input.toLowerCase()
    const negativePattern =
        /\b(wtf|wth|ffs|omfg|shit(ty|tiest)?|dumbass|horrible|awful|piss(ed|ing)? off|piece of (shit|crap|junk)|what the (fuck|hell)|fucking? (broken|useless|terrible|awful|horrible)|fuck you|screw (this|you)|so frustrating|this sucks|damn it)\b/

    return negativePattern.test(lowerInput)
}
```

I have unofficially dubbed this: ***The F\*\*\* Index&trade;***

You're telling me Claude can navigate repositories, edit files, run commands, commission large scale workflows, summon sub-agents, and yet, somewhere inside all of that is a small regex utility function whose sole goal is to answer the question:

 > Did the user just swear at me?

Agent performance telemetry via regex-based sentiment analysis is a sentence I never thought I would say, and yet here we are. Worse still, it kind of makes sense?


## Does This Mean I Can't Swear at Claude Anymore?

No. By all means, continue to swear your heart out.

The sole purpose of this function is to capture cheap user satisfaction analytics, which sheds light on a problem that every AI product eventually encounters, yet one that few people fully appreciate: how do you know whether the AI is doing a good job?

The function itself returns a boolean and is tied to an analytics event:

```TypeScript
    const isNegative = matchesNegativeKeyword(userPromptText)
    const isKeepGoing = matchesKeepGoingKeyword(userPromptText)

    logEvent('tengu_input_prompt', {
        is_negative: isNegative,
        is_keep_going: isKeepGoing,
    })
```

The exact profanity used doesn't even matter as it gets reduced down to a `true`/`false` value by the time it gets logged, making this whole flow feel almost weirdly low-tech given who this codebase belongs to.


## Regex? In This Economy?

My immediate reaction to seeing regex being used to match profanities was "wow I can make this so much more sophisticated, they should totally hire me", but in retrospect, this actually a sensible engineering decision.

Right off the bat, there is no non-determinism with regex. It's cheap, fast, doesn't require intermediate inference calls, doesn't consume any precious tokens, and can't suddenly decide that the word 'awful' feels positive today.

From traversing this and similar code paths, we can infer that the goal is to collect a rough behaviour signal, for which invoking another LLM would have been a pretty expensive means of doing so.

That being said, due to the rudimentary nature of this function, there may be problems deciding what the boolean output means.

A user might swear at Claude because it broke something, or because Claude pleasantly surprised them, or maybe they were just having a bad day.

Some people swear constantly. Others remain exceptionally polite while watching an agent completely dismantle their codebase one line at a time.

Hence, this detector records observable facts, but does not really capture the full story.


## Keep Going, Claude

You will notice in the above code snippet another function called `matchesKeepGoingKeyword`, which also produces a similar boolean event:

```TypeScript
export function matchesKeepGoingKeyword(input: string): boolean {
    const lowerInput = input.toLowerCase().trim()

    // Match "continue" only if it's the entire prompt
    if (lowerInput === 'continue') {
        return true
    }

    // Match "keep going" or "go on" anywhere in the input
    const keepGoingPattern = /\b(keep going|go on)\b/
    return keepGoingPattern.test(lowerInput)
}
```

This function detects three forms of continuation:

 1. A prompt exclusively containing the word `continue`
 1. A prompt containing the phrase `keep going` anywhere inside it
 1. A prompt containing the phrase `go on` anywhere inside it
 
Curiously, the strict regex rules mean that `continue!` followed by an exclamation point, or any punctuation for that matter will not trigger a match because `continue` must be the entire trimmed input.

My intuition tells me this as a metric to track agent autonomy, with the goal being to reduce the amount of dependence on a human in the loop (HITL), however without visibility into the analytics processing backend, we cannot say for sure.

Why would agent autonomy need to be tracked and optimized? Well, imagine this scenario with two different coding agents:

The first receives a task, inspects the repository, calls some tools, edits the relevant files, runs the tests and returns with a completed result.

The second inspects the repository, then waits

The user types: `continue`

It makes the necessary edits, and then pauses again: `continue`

It runs the test suite, then waits again: `continue`

Eventually, it completes the same task, just several conversation turns later.

Both agents technically succeeded, but they did not provide the same experience. One completed the workflow autonomously, while the other required the user to stand behind it with a rolled up newspaper.

As workflows get larger and more complex, lessening the dependence on HITL is a crucial step for successful task execution. However, this shouldn't be taken to the opposite extreme, where agents start handling important design and architectural decisions without consulting the user (see my [other blog post](https://arnavjagetia.com/blog/systems-design-2026) on this matter).

Therefore, a balance must be struck between autonomy and control, and this serves as a reasonable proxy to measure that. 


## Why Operational Metrics Aren't Enough To Measure an Agent's Success

Claude Code's system records plenty of conventional operational data. Their API calls have success and error events, tool calls have durations, result sizes and failure states, sessions accumulate token use, cost, elapsed time, lines changed. Etc.

Even the reason for terminating the user-query loop is logged:

```TypeScript
/** Terminal transition — the query loop returned. */
export type Terminal = {
    reason:
    | 'completed'
    | 'blocking_limit'
    | 'image_error'
    | 'model_error'
    | 'aborted_streaming'
    | 'aborted_tools'
    | 'prompt_too_long'
    | 'stop_hook_prevented'
    | 'hook_stopped'
    | 'max_turns'
    | (string & {})
    error?: unknown
}
```

But while all of this is useful, none of it proves that the users' desired end goal was achieved.

This is where, short of dedicated benchmarks and evaluation pipelines, measuring the performance of an AI agent is difficult. An API endpoint can often be judged by whether it returned the expected response, but an agent can return a perfectly valid response, modify several files, execute every command successfully and still not solve the problem.


## Mo Telemetry Mo Problems

Properly collecting telemetry at scale is only half of the challenge. A data lake full of ***F\*\*\* Index&trade;*** events is still just data. Turning that data into useful information that can drive decisions requires an entirely separate layer of processing and analysis.

An increase in `is_negative` events over a given period is not a particularly deep insight, and on its own could be explained by a million different things.

There is unlikely to be a single metric strong enough to say whether an AI agent did a good job or not. Instead, Claude Code and similar production systems collect dozens of partial signals: completions, retries, failures, corrections, interruptions, permission decisions, surveys, continuation prompts, and moments when the user’s vocabulary becomes noticeably more 'colourful'.

Once the collated data is cleaned, normalized and validated, more sophisticated techniques can then be layered on top, for example, anomaly detection to identify unexpected changes, statistical analysis to separate actual shifts from random noise, and predictive models to estimate qualities that cannot be observed directly.

The original events are slowly transformed into higher level metrics that attempt to describe things such as reliability, autonomy and user satisfaction.

However, this transformation from data into information is straightforward, as key decisions have to be made about which events to include, how they are grouped and normalized, and what period they are measured across. The outcomes of which can completely change the story that the data tells.

The same collection of logs can support many different conclusions, and can be shaped to make analyses all paint a certain picture, either intentionally or unintentionally.

Both of these keyword detectors therefore, don't directly measure Claude's performance. Instead, they produce one small input into a much larger analytical system.

Their value depends on everything that happens afterwards; the quality of the data pipeline, the computations ran, the assumptions behind those computations and, ultimately, the judgement of the people interpreting the results.

Sometimes, measuring intelligent systems requires sophisticated evaluation frameworks, model-based graders and carefully designed benchmarks.

And other times, it starts by checking whether someone typed:

 > *wtf*
